.TH PHOTOREC 8 @TESTDISKDATE@ "Administration Tools"
.SH NAME
photorec \- Recover lost files from harddisk, digital camera and cdrom
.SH SYNOPSIS
.BI "photorec [/log] [/debug] [/d recup_dir] [device|image.dd|image.e01]
.sp
.BI "photorec /version
.SH DESCRIPTION
   \fBPhotoRec\fP is file data recovery software designed to recover lost files including video, documents and archives from Hard Disks and CDRom and lost pictures (Photo Recovery) from digital camera memory. PhotoRec ignores the filesystem and goes after the underlying data, so it'll work even if your media's filesystem is severely damaged or formatted. PhotoRec is safe to use, it will never attempt to write to the drive or memory support you are about to recover lost data from.
   For more information on how to use, please visit the wiki pages on www.cgsecurity.org
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B /log
create a photorec.log file
.TP
.B /debug
add debug information
.SH IMAGE MINIMUMS
The Options screen provides independent minimum image width, height, total
pixels (width times height), and recovered file size in bytes. A value of zero
(the default) disables that minimum. An image must meet every enabled minimum;
a value exactly equal to a minimum passes. The filters apply to JPEG and PNG
images, and do not impose a size limit on other recovered file types. Dimension
limits reject known below-minimum dimensions; unknown or corrupt metadata,
or exhaustion of the bounded probe (16 MiB or 65536 markers), preserves normal
recovery. The byte limit uses the final size determined by normal file checks.
Where contiguous image metadata establishes a below-minimum result, the file
is skipped before creation. Incomplete or fragmented files may require writing
before their final size or dimensions can be checked.
.PP
Batch commands set the same values within the options command. For example:
.PP
.nf
photorec /d recovered /cmd image.dd options,image_min_width,640,image_min_height,480,image_min_pixels,307200,image_min_bytes,10240,search
.fi
.PP
Each value must be an unsigned decimal integer between 0 and
18446744073709551615, without signs, spaces, fractions, or unit suffixes.
Invalid or missing values stop the command before recovery starts. Interrupted
recovery sessions save these options for the normal resume workflow.
.PP
QPhotoRec starts with all image minimums disabled; its graphical interface
does not expose these settings.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR testdisk (8),
.BR fdisk (8).
.BR
.SH AUTHOR
PhotoRec @VERSION@, Data Recovery Utility, @TESTDISKDATE@
